+ * 3117, 3119: let the Wi-Fi hardware sleep, and with it the modem its firmware runs in. Found on the phone, 2026-11-22
+ * (the power controller's table at 0x0c3f008c says whether the modem sleeps): with every engine's copy-complete
+ * interrupt enable (CE_HOST_IE) at 0, as this polled driver left it, the hardware never sleeps; with the enables at 1
+ * and no status (CE_HOST_IS) standing it sleeps within a second. So the enable is the host's "I am not servicing this
+ * engine" and a standing status its "I still have to": ath10k keeps an engine's interrupt masked exactly while its
+ * NAPI poll runs, clears the status before handling, and unmasks at the end. ath10k ce.c: "When the last copy engine
+ * interrupt is cleared the hardware will go to sleep. Once this happens any access to the CE registers can cause a
+ * hardware fault." With kern.sweet_wlan_ce_sleep=1 (one way: a reboot ends it) this driver does the same without
+ * interrupts, from what it sees in memory:
+ *  - a destination ring whose next descriptor is filled has a completion standing, so the hardware is awake: enable to
+ *    0, then status cleared and descriptors handled until a pass finds none, then enable to 1;
+ *  - a source ring whose read index in memory (ath10k_ce_src_ring_read_index_from_ddr: the pool's first words, CTRL1
+ *    UPD in wce_up) has caught up with what was sent has its completion standing: enable 0, status cleared, enable 1.
